<Layout pageTitle="Tissue mechanics — methods & validation" description="Equations, assumptions, numerical verification, H01 provenance and staged validation requirements for the experimental tissue mechanics laboratories.">
<article>
<p><a href="/simulations/tissue-mechanics/">Brain Tissue Mechanics Laboratory</a> · <a href="/simulations/cellular-mechanics/">Cellular Tissue Mechanics Laboratory</a></p>
<h2>Release 01: a verified elastic baseline, not a validated brain model</h2>
<p>The two pages share a finite-strain tetrahedral finite-element engine. The render mesh and H01 anatomy follow its computed displacement field. Quasistatic equilibrium is found by limited-memory BFGS with an energy-decreasing line search and incremental prescribed loading. Iterations and their visual replay are not physical time. A worker enforces a 25-second elapsed solve budget, excluding anatomy download.</p>
<h3>Constitutive law and units</h3>
<p>For deformation gradient F and local volume ratio J = det F, the strain-energy density is:</p>
<pre>W(F) = μ/2 [tr(FᵀF) − 3] − μ ln J + λ/2 (ln J)²
λ = 2 μ ν / (1 − 2 ν)
P = μ F + (λ ln J − μ) F⁻ᵀ
σ = P Fᵀ / J
E = (FᵀF − I) / 2</pre>
<p>Internal lengths are millimetres, stresses are kilopascals, forces are millinewtons and energies are microjoules. Thus 1 kPa × 1 mm² = 1 mN. The displayed Green-strain norm is the Frobenius norm of E, not a principal strain or injury probability. Von Mises stress is a visualization measure, not a validated neural damage criterion.</p>
<p>The default shear modulus is an illustrative 0.8 kPa within the 0.4–1.4 kPa range reported by <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27989920/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Budday et al. (2017), Mechanical characterization of human brain tissue</a>. That study found a modified Ogden law necessary to capture the combined loading response. Our neo-Hookean baseline is not their fitted model. The default Poisson ratio of 0.45 is a numerical regularization choice, not a measured specimen value. The 0.49 setting exposes incompressibility sensitivity; first-order displacement tetrahedra can lock.</p>
<h3>Boundary conditions and contact</h3>
<p>Shear fixes the lower face and prescribes all upper-face displacement components. Compression and stretch clamp the base but prescribe only the upper vertical displacement. Grabbing translates a finite surface patch. The gently curved cap changes the stress-free geometry only; it does not add an anatomical pia membrane.</p>
<p>Spherical indentation uses frictionless penalty contact sampled at reference-area-weighted upper-surface nodes. The penalty stiffness is 100 μ/L by default. Penalty overlap is explicitly reported. No pia rupture, cutting, friction or puncture threshold is implemented. Indentation reaction forces are substantially mesh-sensitive on the currently exposed resolutions.</p>
<p>Micromotion uses a 250 × 250 µm rigid cuboid in an existing tract of 250, 500 or 750 µm depth. The outer sides and bottom of the sample are clamped; the tract boundary is perfectly bonded to the prescribed implant displacement. Changing depth rebuilds the unloaded tract; it does not simulate insertion. The implanted geometry is a generic mechanical test object, not a Neuropixels specification. The coarse mesh is disabled for this mode because it leaves no useful interior relaxation degrees of freedom.</p>
<p><a href="https://doi.org/10.1088/1741-2560/2/4/006" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Subbaroyan, Martin &amp; Kipke (2005)</a> and <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8770436/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Al Abed et al. (2022)</a> motivate studying implant/tissue micromotion, but their published models have not been reproduced or used to validate this implementation.</p>
<h3>Numerical verification and its limits</h3>
<p>The automated tests include reference volume and oriented surface closure, zero rest force, finite rigid-rotation invariance, constitutive and assembled force/energy gradients, analytic shear and dilation, affine deformation patches, fixed boundary enforcement, force/unit scaling, topology-preserving tract refinement, positive Jacobians, explicit nonconvergence classification, load continuation, native-coordinate H01 cropping and exact affine anatomy advection.</p>
<p>The normalized free-force residual is ||g_free||₂ / (μ L²). Standard equilibrium tolerance is 10⁻⁵; “Refine equilibrium” tightens it to 2 × 10⁻⁶ without changing the selected mesh. A positive-volume guard rejects J ≤ 0.08. This is a numerical safeguard, not a tissue failure criterion. “Converged” means the finite system satisfies this optimizer criterion; it does not mean a mesh-independent or experimentally validated result.</p>
<p>Pre-release mesh sweeps reveal appreciable changes in indentation and implant reaction forces. No present contact preset is certified mesh-converged. Independent FEM comparisons, mixed or locking-resistant elements, experimental material calibration and contact validation are required before quantitative design conclusions. Hardware-specific timing is shown in the scene; no universal real-time performance guarantee is made.</p>
<h3>H01 anatomy and cellular interpretation</h3>
<p>The cellular page reuses the site's attributed H01 proofread reference pack. Its twelve selected skeletons originate from <a href="https://h01-release.storage.googleapis.com/data.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">the H01 release</a>, described by <a href="https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.adk4858" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Shapson-Coe et al. (2024)</a>. Credit: Lichtman Laboratory, Harvard University, and Connectomics at Google; CC BY 4.0, as recorded in the source manifest.</p>
<p>Each 80 µm crop is centered on a selected source soma coordinate. All included neurites retain native relative positions. The source coordinates are translated only: no isotropic rescaling, tissue-layer reassignment, rotation to M1/S1 or population filling is performed. Existing source simplification has a 0.7 µm positional tolerance. Cylindrical surfaces interpolate recorded skeleton radii; overly large soma-skeleton radii are omitted rather than presented as segmented membranes. Crop boundaries are clipped with radius clearance, omitting partial boundary cross-sections.</p>
<p>This is sparse EM-derived skeleton anatomy, not a dense segmented volume of neuropil. There are no reconstructed membrane meshes, complete cell bodies, extracellular gaps, independent cellular materials or fluid mechanics in the supplied sample. Anatomy is barycentrically embedded in the tissue reference mesh; each surface vertex follows the material displacement. Applying a homogenized tissue law at this microscopic scale is an uncalibrated assumption. Displayed surrounding tissue strain is not a validated cell-specific membrane strain or injury measurement.</p>
<h3>Import a genuine segmented surface</h3>
<p>The cellular page accepts a local JSON file with the following schema. Coordinates must already lie inside the sample: x and z in [−40, 40] µm, y in [0, 80] µm. Geometry outside the mechanics domain is rejected, not silently clamped or rescaled. The budget is 100,000 vertices, 300,000 triangles and a 15 MB file. Provenance is required but remains user-supplied.</p>
<pre>{`{
  "schema": "bts-cellular-surface-v1",
  "dataset": "dataset and exact region identifier",
  "sourceUrl": "https://source.example/data",
  "license": "the applicable data license",
  "units": "um",
  "positions": [0, 20, 0, 1, 20, 0, 0, 21, 0],
  "indices": [0, 1, 2],
  "note": "Segmentation version, processing and coordinate transform"
}`}</pre>
<p>The triangle above is a schema example, not biological data. Store source segment identifiers, segmentation version and transforms in the note or dataset identifier. A future dense-EM pipeline must preserve those records at object level.</p>
<h3>Next validation stages</h3>
<p><strong>Stage 2:</strong> introduce a locking-resistant mixed formulation, reproduce independent indentation and implant benchmarks, and fit a study-specific hyperelastic/viscoelastic law. Verify mesh and timestep convergence across loading modes. <strong>Stage 3:</strong> add a distinct pia layer and validated contact, followed separately by puncture/fracture. <strong>Stage 4:</strong> import dense EM membrane meshes and investigate heterogeneous microstructural mechanics; do not infer local material properties from EM geometry alone.</p>
</article>
